    biography: `David G. Long obtained his Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering from the University of Southern California in 1989. From 1983-1990, he worked at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory developing advanced radar remote sensing systems. He served as Project Engineer on the NASA Scatterometer (NSCAT) project and managed the SCANSCAT/SeaWinds projects across multiple satellite missions.

He joined BYU in 1990. He is the director of the BYU Center for Remote Sensing and served as Associate Dean from 2012 to 2016. He leads multiple NASA-sponsored research projects in remote sensing, has published over 450 works in signal processing, radar scatterometry, and synthetic aperture radar, and received multiple NASA Certificates of Recognition. He holds IEEE Life Fellow status and serves as Associate Editor for IEEE Geoscience and Remote Sensing Letters.`,
